{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793742836,"nonce":"CPxWYkXMaROxys5prSkjXNjF+ZI=","title":"Deploy the RayDE landing page with a working Pocket Studio editor","message":"Deploy rayde.org/www.rayde.org with the responsive landing page and a working embedded editor. app.rayde.org remains the signed-in standalone workspace product. The landing editor must load independently of workspace provisioning, expose clear asynchronous readiness, and provide real built-in extensions and editable Haxe/RayScript examples through an isolated remote demo session.\n\nPreview decision confirmed by the user on 2026-11-03: the landing demo may use the cached WASM route. Ordinary workspace previews currently run on the session host and stream pixels; preserve that path. Do not replace streaming globally or silently choose a different runner. Current RunPart.previewAppBuild selects the Ashui surface path before AppPreviewBuild, so the landing demo needs an explicit Run Configuration choice that this dispatch respects.\n\nInitial experience: serve an immutable Pocket Studio source release and its matching precompiled WASM preview from public asset storage; render immediately without Haxe, Ash, Rayzor warm-up or compute admission on the critical path. Editor Main.hx and BoardApp.hx remain side by side, sidebar collapsed, landing traffic lights retained. Smaller/unsupported browsers receive the existing static capture and usable site. Provision the remote demo session asynchronously, reconcile browser edits through the existing session/CRDT model, and activate real LSP/extensions as it becomes ready. No shared writable demo workspace between visitors.\n\nAfter edits: keep the current picture visible with its build state until a preview matching the current inputs is ready. Validate the Haxe/bytecode/WASM cache against source, assets, manifests, macros, library versions, compiler/runtime, flags and relevant environment. Reuse an exact hit; build changed inputs only inside the visitor sandbox. Never present the shipped preview as current for modified source. Serve app artifacts on the separate preview origin, retaining the scoped file bridge and input handling.\n\nDeployment order: (1) verify cached demo Run Configuration and artifact provenance locally; (2) produce a prepared Linux demo toolchain/session image and private warm caches; (3) add bounded anonymous demo leases to the Rust gateway/runner; (4) stage the entire landing experience on its own site hostname with isolated app/assets/preview routes; (5) run two-visitor, extension, preview, recovery and budget acceptance checks; (6) promote a verified immutable release to production with rollback. Initial HTML must not wait on any network data request. Production DNS, credentials and new paid compute are separate deployment actions, not performed by filing this plan.\n\nCost model: static editor/app rendering happens in the visitor browser for this demo, eliminating server graphics rendering and pixel-stream egress for its initial preview. Remote session CPU/RAM, LSP/extensions, changed-source compilation, relay activity and retained disks still cost money. Share only immutable artifacts/toolchain data; allocate independent mutable session state. Measure VM memory alongside LSP/extension hosts and compilations before setting concurrency. The present staging host admits only one 3 GiB-capped guest, so it is a pilot rather than public landing capacity.\n\nCloudflare prices checked 2026-11-03: Workers paid minimum is $5/month, including 10 million dynamic requests and 30 million CPU ms. R2 Standard includes 10 GB-month storage and 10 million Class B reads; above that storage is $0.015/GB-month and Class B reads $0.36/million; R2 Internet egress is free. These are edge/storage terms, not a complete hosting quote. DO active duration, storage, third-party compute and networking are separate; current persistent upstream relay must be measured rather than assumed to hibernate. Proposed pilot budget = edge subscription + eligible usage overages + KVM compute host + backup/storage. Choose a KVM-capable host only after memory/CPU/concurrency evidence; do not infer nested virtualization from a cheap VPS price.\n\nSources:\nhttps://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/pricing/\nhttps://developers.cloudflare.com/r2/pricing/\nhttps://developers.cloudflare.com/durable-objects/platform/pricing/\n\nRelated: staging deployment 52138fcda83502ec87679713955f7cf7c7f7e8654a0ef55ba60357bd0f22a904; historical playground fe0ad8bbe236cc1389c8b73e2d1a5b4b6440af5c8f26453d96692456da26f95f; framework previews 9c6f60e27c2cb4be92d534eccc95910e303a5c4e03e1e8779c6d52ca9c40a330; Linux software rendering 3d6241f8d5cbe40b5ef19564282c596293c85a1d378083c13caa7fa6f8108818 and 784b568c8a12ffa7cff9ca568d7d71b07f8407260631ec36bb4c4fe410dfbee5. Software rendering remains relevant to streamed workspace previews and is not a landing WASM launch dependency.\n\nDone when: a fresh visitor can open the live landing editor without signing in, edit Haxe and RayScript, use built-in extensions, open the cached demo app, change code and see a correctly rebuilt app, reconnect without cross-visitor state leakage, and receive responsive loading/errors at capacity. Mobile makes no editor/WASM fetch. Production release is reproducible and rollback tested. Measured capacity and cost per active visitor are recorded in the child trackers.","files":null}]}